CORE
main.c
--unwind 2 --rounds 3 --por --allow-pointer-unsoundness --no-assertions --datarace
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
--
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
--
Partial-order reduction dropped schedules that exhibit a data race.

`create_atomic_canonical` constrains every atomic block with

    fire_cond => (ABR v ABW)

so a block may occupy a schedule slot only if one of its reads observes a
value from elsewhere (ABR) or one of its writes is itself observed (ABW).
Both witnesses are built from read-side quantities -- NRP, WINR, LW, LOW --
so a write that no read ever observes is invisible to them. The effect is to
keep one representative per read-from equivalence class, which is sound for
reachability: two schedules that differ only in the order of two writes
nobody reads really do compute the same thing.

A data race is not preserved by that equivalence. It is concurrency of
conflicting accesses, not flow of values, so the two schedules in such a
class can disagree about whether a race occurs -- and in this program they
do. The block holding `datum = 5` has no read, and its write is never
observed, so neither witness can be satisfied and the block was never placed
where it races with main's `datum = 8`. The answer was SUCCESSFUL, against
FAILED under --no-por.

A read on either side restores it: make the racing access a read, or let the
block read `datum` before writing it, and the race is found with POR on.
That is the dividing line -- a read/write conflict leaves a read-from edge
for the witnesses to find, a write/write conflict leaves none -- so the fix
exempts from canonicality exactly those blocks that write an object another
thread also writes. Under --datarace only; the constraint is unchanged for
reachability, where it is sound, so the reduction unreach-call benefits from
is untouched.

On the corpus this is `pthread-ext/semaphore-posix-race`, SUCCESSFUL with POR
and FAILED without, worth -32 as a missed bug against +1 for finding it. The
companion flag is LAZYPO_POR_RACE_EXEMPT (ww, wide, off) for the ablation.
